---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import CardCount from '../components/CardCount.astro';
import Share from '../components/Share.astro';
import FamilyDiagram from '../components/FamilyDiagram.astro';
import ConfigTable from '../components/ConfigTable.astro';
import ProvenanceBar from '../components/ProvenanceBar.astro';
import StartTeaser from '../islands/StartTeaser.tsx';
import { SECTIONS } from '../lib/nav';

const routes = ['Explore', 'Compose a list', 'The dataset', 'Definitions', 'English OpenList'].map(
  (label) => SECTIONS.find((s) => s.label === label)!,
);
---

<Base
  title="Start"
  section="Start"
  description="An inventory of candidate English strings, each with a record of where it came from, and the English OpenList as a dated subset of it."
>
  <div class="frame start">
    <section class="opening" aria-labelledby="title">
      <div class="opening-text">
        <h1 id="title">
          <Figure id="config.universe.rows" /> candidate English strings, each with a record of where it came from
        </h1>
        <p class="thesis">Cut a word list of your own from them, under a definition you choose.</p>
        <dl class="what">
          <div>
            <dt>What this is</dt>
            <dd>
              The CEOL Universe is a free dataset of strings that might be English words: single words and their forms,
              hyphenated compounds, and multiword terms, gathered from dictionaries, word lists, spell-checkers, and a
              large corpus of books. For each string it records which of <CardCount name="published_set_count" /> published
              lists include it, how often it occurs in books where that was measured, and how it entered the dataset. It
              does not decide what counts as a word. You choose the definition, and it gives you the list that meets it,
              with the evidence for every entry.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Why it exists</dt>
            <dd>
              Dictionaries and word lists disagree about what counts as a word. They are updated at different times, they
              cover different fields, some leave out slang or profanity on purpose, and each has its own rules for spaces,
              hyphens, and other marks. The CEOL Universe takes no side: it records which published lists hold each string,
              in versions fixed at a date, so you can see where they agree and where they differ.
              <a href="/about/#why">The About page</a> says more.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>The English OpenList</dt>
            <dd>
              A free English word list made for word games and apps. It began as its own project in 2026; its list at a
              fixed revision is part of this dataset, the <code>eol-valid</code> config, so each of its entries carries the
              same record. Projects built on it include <a href="https://ars-magna.pages.dev/">Ars Magna</a>,
              <a href="https://amordle.vercel.app/">Amordle</a>, and
              <a href="https://ryanjosephkamp.github.io/portfolios/anagram-tiles/">Anagram Tiles</a>.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What they are for</dt>
            <dd>
              A word list for a game, a puzzle, or a spell-checker, cut to your own rules; a vocabulary for software, with
              its sources stated; research on how dictionaries and word lists agree and differ; and test sets for studies
              of how language models handle rare and invented words.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="lede">
          The CEOL Universe is the inventory. The English OpenList is a dated subset of it, and both live here. Neither
          one is a verdict: a row says which sources list the string and how it came in.
        </p>
        <aside class="first-limit" aria-labelledby="limit-title">
          <h2 id="limit-title">Most rows rest on a single corpus</h2>
          <p>
            <Figure id="rows.inherited_google_books_no_published_set" /> rows
            (<Share part="rows.inherited_google_books_no_published_set" whole="rows" />) came in through the English
            OpenList, are listed by none of the published word lists, and are Google Books 1-gram types. For
            <Figure id="rows.inherited_google_books_only" /> of them, Google Books is the only evidence, and it is a corpus
            of scanned books, with recognition errors.
          </p>
          <p class="limit-more">
            The dataset's card states every limitation before anything else: <a href="/dataset/limitations/">read them
            all</a>.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
      <StartTeaser client:load cubeUrl="/data/cube-v1.0.json" />
    </section>

    <section class="family" aria-labelledby="family-title">
      <div class="family-text">
        <h2 id="family-title">One inventory, one subset inside it</h2>
        <p>
          The universe gathers candidates from the English OpenList's own intake, a census of spelling
          classes across published word lists, an audit, and a pass over multiword terms. The English
          OpenList is the
          <Figure id="config.eol-valid.rows" /> rows that its valid list held at revision
          <code>08ae92b8</code>, as this version ships them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The English OpenList is <Share part="config.eol-valid.rows" whole="config.universe.rows" /> of the universe.
          Each of the dataset's configs is a subset of the universe:
        </p>
        <ConfigTable />
      </div>
      <FamilyDiagram />
    </section>

    <section class="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-title">
      <h2 id="evidence-title">What each row rests on</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        Every row is in 1 of 4 provenance classes. A <em>channel</em> is a recorded way the string came in;
        <em>containment</em> means a published word list or a corpus holds it.
      </p>
      <ProvenanceBar />
    </section>

    <section class="routes" aria-labelledby="routes-title">
      <h2 id="routes-title">Where to go next</h2>
      <ul>
        {
          routes.map((r) => (
            <li class:list={[{ later: !r.built }]}>
              {r.built ? <a href={r.href}>{r.label}</a> : <span class="route-label">{r.label}</span>}
              <p>{r.blurb}</p>
              {!r.built && <p class="route-later">In the next build.</p>}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .start {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  .opening {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6) var(--space-7);
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .opening {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 29rem);
    }
  }
  .opening-text {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 40rem;
  }
  .opening h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-5);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .thesis {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.3;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .lede {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .what {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-block: var(--space-4);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .what div {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-1);
  }
  .what dt {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .what dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .first-limit {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .first-limit h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-weight: 650;
  }
  .first-limit p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  .limit-more {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .family {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .family {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    }
  }
  .family-text {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .family h2,
  .evidence h2,
  .routes h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
  }

  .evidence {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }
  .section-lede {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .routes {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }
  .routes ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 14rem), 1fr));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .routes li {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-4) var(--space-5) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .routes a,
  .route-label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .routes a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .routes li p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 32ch;
  }
  .routes li.later .route-label {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .route-later {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
</style>
